      "status": "affected",
      "action_statement": "No fixed release is available yet; update when Docker Hardened Images publishes one.",
      "status_notes": "CVE-2026-25541 (GHSA-434x-w66g-qw3r, RUSTSEC-2026-0007): in the Rust bytes crate 1.2.1 through 1.11.0, BytesMut::reserve adds new_cap and offset without an overflow check in its reclaim path, so an overflowing reservation can leave the buffer recording more capacity than was allocated and let later calls create out-of-bounds slices. It is fixed in bytes 1.11.1 by commit d0293b0: https://github.com/tokio-rs/bytes/security/advisories/GHSA-434x-w66g-qw3r, https://rustsec.org/advisories/RUSTSEC-2026-0007.html, https://github.com/tokio-rs/bytes/commit/d0293b0e35838123c51ca5dfdf468ecafee4398f. Scanned every package of this owner published in the DHI Alpine 3.23 repository, for x86_64 and aarch64: all 17 packages (rust, cargo, rustc-dev, rustfmt, rust-clippy, rust-dbg, rust-src, rust-wasm, rust-uefi, rust-gdb, rust-lldb and the doc and completion packages) of builds 1.91.1-r0, 1.91.1-r3 and 1.91.1-r4, and build 1.91.1-r2, which publishes only rust and cargo. Every file, including the .rlib archives and the .rmeta compiler metadata, was searched for the crate's source paths, which name the crate version it was built from. The cargo program (/usr/bin/cargo) in every one of those builds, on both architectures, is compiled with bytes 1.10.1, whose src/bytes_mut.rs still has the unchecked v_capacity >= new_cap + offset test, and the published cargo debug symbols (rust-dbg, x86_64 and aarch64, 1.91.1-r0, -r3 and -r4) contain BytesMut::reserve_inner, the function the fix changes. Apart from cargo and its debug symbols in rust-dbg, no file in these packages contains the bytes crate's source paths. No published build of this owner carries a fixed bytes version, so every published build is affected."
    },
